================================================================================
1.0  EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
================================================================================

The digital transformation of Africa is a sociological phenomenon rooted in 
collaborative engineering. Africa's Talking (AT) provides the critical "metal"
—the APIs—that enable this.

This document establishes the Africa's Talking Community Code of Conduct (CoC).
It is not a list of social niceties; it is a Professional Standard of Merit.
Drawing from MIT Open Source Principles, it prioritizes:

  1. Freedom of Innovation: Unrestricted exchange of ideas/code.
  2. Limitation of Liability: Code is shared "as is"; responsibility is personal.
  3. Universal Dignity: Rejection of identity politics in favor of high 
     professional standards for all.

This framework aligns strictly with the Terms of Service (ToS), AML obligations
(Airtime/Data), and Data Protection protocols.

================================================================================
2.0  STRATEGIC FOUNDATIONS
================================================================================

2.1  MISSION AND MERITOCRACY
     The mission is to empower developers to create and sustain great businesses.

     > MERITOCRACY
       Status is derived from code quality, contribution utility, and 
       helpfulness—not demographics.

     > TOOL AGNOSTICISM
       We are indifferent to the stack (Python, Java, PHP, Go, cURL). We value 
       the outcome: solving problems efficiently.

     > PROFESSIONALISM
       Participation is a privilege. Whether in Nairobi, Lagos, or remote, 
       members act with the rigor of a high-stakes engineering team.

2.2  LEGAL INTERFACE
     Social misconduct signals technical risk. The CoC acts as the "social API" 
     for the legal ToS.

